-- title:
--   Theorem 4 — constant-depth 1D Clifford generators (corrected: vector-first Kazhdan)
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge1$ be the number of qubits on an open nearest-neighbor line, and let $\mathrm{Cl}_n$ be the full Clifford group generated by Hadamard, phase and CNOT gates, retaining its discrete central phases. There are a single effective program, a natural depth bound $D$, and real constants $\kappa>0$ and $0<\Delta\le1$, all independent of $n$ and of the representation, with the following property. The program outputs a finite list of ancilla-free Clifford circuits whose implemented matrices form one set $S_n\subseteq\mathrm{Cl}_n$. This same set satisfies
--
--   $$
--   |S_n|\le451,\qquad S_n^{-1}=S_n,\qquad
--   \langle S_n\rangle=\mathrm{Cl}_n,\qquad
--   K(\mathrm{Cl}_n;S_n)\ge\kappa,
--   $$
--
--   and every output circuit has depth at most $D$. The Kazhdan lower bound means that for every complex Hilbert-space unitary representation $\sigma$ with no nonzero invariant vector and every unit vector $v$, some $s\in S_n$ satisfies $\|\sigma(s)v-v\|\ge\kappa$. In addition, for every natural dimension $d$ and every unitary representation $\rho:\mathrm{Cl}_n\to U(d)$ with no nonzero invariant vector,
--
--   $$
--   \left\|\frac{1}{|S_n\cup\{I\}|}
--   \sum_{g\in S_n\cup\{I\}}\rho(g)\right\|_{2\to2}
--   \le 1-\Delta.
--   $$
--
--   This is the companion theorem giving a uniformly bounded family of shallow Clifford generators with both expansion guarantees. It retains all positive system sizes, including $n=1$, and requires explicitness, generation, symmetry, depth and both lower bounds for the same set. The existing base lemmas remain separate open proof targets, not assumptions of this statement.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The published circuit language uses the computational basis on exactly $n$ physical qubits, disjoint layers and adjacent two-qubit gates, with no periodic edge or auxiliary qubits. Its Clifford gates synthesize CZ and Y with a uniform constant depth overhead. Lists encode effective implementations; the spectral average uses the actual set, with identity adjoined and duplicates removed. The published vector-first Kazhdan predicate corrects the quantifier order of Definition 4. The rank-one correction to Lemma 16 concerns a supporting decomposition only; it imposes no rank restriction here.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: Definition 4 places the generator before the unit vector → the published vector-first KazhdanLower, where the generator may depend on the unit vector; reason: for G=(Z/2)^2 and S={a,b}, the representation rho(a)=diag(-1,1,-1), rho(b)=diag(1,-1,-1) has no invariant vector but each generator fixes a unit vector; the printed constant is zero, contradicting Eq. (22), whereas the vector-first constant is sqrt(2) and the average squared-displacement constant is 2
--   - encoding: uniform depth O(1) and Kazhdan and spectral-gap lower bounds Omega(1) → one natural depth bound D and positive real lower bounds kappa and Delta, with Delta at most 1, chosen before every n, dimension d and representation rho; reason: these are uniform asymptotic bounds; Delta can always be decreased to min(Delta,1) without changing any source instance
--   - encoding: an explicit generating set of Clifford gates → a fixed partial-recursive program outputs a finite list of circuit codes from input (n,0,0), and ImplementsClSet identifies its image with the same set S; reason: this expresses effective construction without asserting a classical runtime bound; repeated implementations do not change set cardinality or uniform weights
--   - encoding: single-qubit Paulis, H, S, CZ and CNOT in constant nearest-neighbor depth → the published legal layered open-line circuits with primitive H, S, X, Z, CNOT and S-inverse; reason: CZ is (I tensor H) CNOT (I tensor H), Y is S X S-inverse and S-inverse is S cubed; these exact identities preserve every generator and change depth by only a uniform factor
--   - encoding: the representation essential norm subtracts the invariant projector from the uniform average on S union {I} → clRepresentationNorm S rho is the Euclidean operator norm of the uniform average over insert 1 S when NoInvariant rho holds; reason: the invariant projector is zero on precisely the representation domain in Theorem 4; insert removes duplicates and makes the averaging set nonempty
-- source:
--   Guoding Liu, Jonas Helsen, STOC 2027 submission #221 (October 2026), p.6, Theorem 4, Eq. (4); Definition 3 and corrected Definition 4, p.11, Eqs. (16),(20); proof pp.36–47, especially Lemma 23 and Eq. (269). https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/221.pdf

import Definitions.Def_LiuHelsenDesigns_OneD_circuits
import Definitions.Def_LiuHelsenDesigns_OneD_designs
import Definitions.Def_LiuHelsenDesigns_OneD_groups
import Definitions.Def_LiuHelsenDesigns_OneD_kazhdanBounds
import Definitions.Def_LiuHelsenDesigns_OneD_localLaws

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LiuHelsenDesigns.OneD.Supplement

/-- Theorem 4, p.6, Eq. (4), with all properties on the same explicit set.
The published `KazhdanLower` corrects Definition 4's printed quantifier order:
the generator may depend on the unit vector. All constants precede the qubit
count and the representation. The spectral average adjoins the identity once.
The published circuit language synthesizes Y and CZ in constant Clifford depth. -/
theorem theorem_4 :
    ∃ (code : Nat.Partrec.Code) (D : ℕ) (κ Δ : ℝ),
      0 < κ ∧ 0 < Δ ∧ Δ ≤ 1 ∧
      ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∃ cs : List Circuit, ∃ S : Finset (Cl n),
        ProgramOutputs code n 0 0 cs ∧ ImplementsClSet cs S ∧
        S.card ≤ 451 ∧ InverseClosed (S : Set (Cl n)) ∧
        Subgroup.closure (S : Set (Cl n)) = ⊤ ∧
        (∀ C ∈ cs, depth C ≤ D) ∧
        KazhdanLower (Cl n) (S : Set (Cl n)) κ ∧
        ∀ d : ℕ, ∀ ρ : Cl n →* Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin d) ℂ,
          NoInvariant ρ → clRepresentationNorm S ρ ≤ 1 - Δ := by sorry

end LiuHelsenDesigns.OneD.Supplement
